### Why is the front pop valid?

When `prefix[i] - prefix[front] >= k`, the span from `front` to `i` qualifies. Any future `i' > i` would give a longer span with the same `front`, so this left endpoint can never produce a shorter answer. We record the length and pop it permanently.

### Why is the back pop valid?

If `prefix[back] >= prefix[i]`, then the back index is a worse left endpoint than `i` for any future right endpoint: it is earlier (giving a longer span) and has a higher or equal prefix (giving a smaller difference). So it is strictly dominated and can be removed.

### Why is this O(N)?

Each index is pushed onto the deque exactly once and popped at most once (either from the front or the back). So the total deque operations are at most 2N, and the prefix-sum computation is N. Total time is **O(N)**.

### What are the edge cases?

Several edge cases require attention.

- **No qualifying subarray**: return -1 when even the total sum is less than k.
- **Single element >= k**: the answer is 1.
- **All negatives**: impossible to reach a positive k, return -1.
- **k = 0**: every non-empty subarray with non-negative sum qualifies; the answer is 1 if any non-negative element exists.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ShortestSubarrayEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(shortest(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 100));   // -1
        System.out.println(shortest(new int[]{50000}, 50000));    // 1
        System.out.println(shortest(new int[]{-1, -2, -3}, 1));  // -1
    }

    static int shortest(int[] arr, int k) {
        int n = arr.length; long[] p = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i + 1] = p[i] + arr[i];
        java.util.Deque<Integer> dq = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            while (!dq.isEmpty() && p[i] - p[dq.peekFirst()] >= k) best = Math.min(best, i - dq.pollFirst());
            while (!dq.isEmpty() && p[dq.peekLast()] >= p[i]) dq.pollLast();
            dq.addLast(i);
        }
        return best == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : best;
    }
}
```

